WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.983
The Mediterranean Sea is bordered by orange trees and rugged mountains.

2
00:00:04.984 --> 00:00:09.150
The air is pure and perfumed with the scent of myrtle.

3
00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:14.626
But Captain Nemo was in a hurry, so I experienced none of the Mediterranean's enchantments.

4
00:00:14.627 --> 00:00:23.610
Starting from the shores of Greece on the morning of February 16, we traveled at full speed and reached the Atlantic Ocean 48 hours later.

5
00:00:23.611 --> 00:00:28.951
It was obvious to me that Captain Nemo wanted to avoid the countries surrounding the Mediterranean.

6
00:00:28.952 --> 00:00:34.356
The breeze probably brought back too many memories, and perhaps too many regrets.

7
00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:39.039
We were traveling so quickly that Ned had to give up any hope of escape.

8
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.943
It would have been impossible to launch the small boat when we were traveling thirty-five to forty feet per second.

9
00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:48.284
He might as well have jumped off a train going at full speed.

10
00:00:48.285 --> 00:00:55.935
I followed our route on the gallery map and calculated that we had traveled ten thousand leagues in three and a half months.

11
00:00:55.936 --> 00:01:03.253
Later in the morning of February 18, the Nautilus rose to the surface, so we could take our daily walk upon the platform.

12
00:01:03.254 --> 00:01:07.086
I went up at once, accompanied by Ned and Conseil.

13
00:01:07.087 --> 00:01:13.324
But the Atlantic Ocean was so rough that the submarine rocked violently with every wave that crashed over it.

14
00:01:13.325 --> 00:01:19.435
We soon decided to go below. Conseil returned to his cabin while Ned followed me to mine.

15
00:01:19.436 --> 00:01:23.491
When my door was shut, he sat down and looked at me silently.

16
00:01:23.492 --> 00:01:30.411
"Ned, my friend," I said, "I know how disappointed you must be that we couldn't escape while in the Mediterranean."

17
00:01:30.412 --> 00:01:37.721
The harpooner did not respond, yet the crease in his brow and the expression on his face showed me that he was thinking hard.

18
00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:43.816
"I don't think we should give up yet," I continued. "We're going up the coast of Portugal.

19
00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:49.554
France and England are not far away, and we can easily find refuge in one of those countries.

20
00:01:49.555 --> 00:01:56.229
Now if the Nautilus were traveling south, I'd share your uneasiness, but I think you'll be able to act in a few days."

21
00:01:56.230 --> 00:02:01.983
Ned stared at me for a few more moments before he spoke. "We're leaving tonight."

22
00:02:01.984 --> 00:02:05.776
I was so startled by this statement that words failed me.

23
00:02:05.777 --> 00:02:11.207
"We agreed to wait for an opportunity," Ned went on, "and that opportunity has arrived.

24
00:02:11.208 --> 00:02:14.459
Tonight we'll be only a few miles from the Spanish coast.

25
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:18.284
It's cloudy and windy—good conditions for an escape."

26
00:02:18.285 --> 00:02:22.268
As I was still silent, Ned stood up and faced me.

27
00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:28.173
"We leave tonight at nine o'clock," he said. "I've already told Conseil to be ready.

28
00:02:28.174 --> 00:02:32.355
At that time Captain Nemo will be in his room, probably asleep.

29
00:02:32.356 --> 00:02:38.434
Conseil and I will go up the central staircase, and you'll remain nearby in the library, awaiting my signal.

30
00:02:38.435 --> 00:02:46.339
The oars, mast, and sail are already in the small boat, and I've even gotten us some provisions. So everything is ready."

31
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.411
"But the sea is so rough," I said, finally finding my voice.

32
00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:56.577
"I agree, but we have a sturdy boat and we'll be traveling only a few miles.

33
00:02:56.578 --> 00:02:59.482
Who knows where the Nautilus will be tomorrow?

34
00:02:59.483 --> 00:03:07.140
We could be hundreds of leagues away, so we have to take this chance tonight. I will see you later, at nine o'clock."

35
00:03:07.141 --> 00:03:11.577
With these words Ned departed, leaving me feeling numb.

36
00:03:11.578 --> 00:03:17.966
I had always imagined that when the time came to escape, there would be an opportunity to discuss it.

37
00:03:17.967 --> 00:03:22.482
But Ned had given me no time to think, never mind talk, over the matter. I

38
00:03:22.483 --> 00:03:26.388
knew he was right; this was a good opportunity to escape. But

39
00:03:26.389 --> 00:03:29.633
it was still hard to believe that the moment had arrived. At

40
00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:34.363
that point I heard the sounds that meant the submarine was sinking under the waves. I

41
00:03:34.364 --> 00:03:41.283
passed the rest of the day torn between the thrill of freedom and the sadness of abandoning my underwater studies. Sometimes

42
00:03:41.284 --> 00:03:49.362
I hoped our escape would succeed, and other times I wished something would happen to prevent us from leaving the submarine. Twice

43
00:03:49.363 --> 00:03:54.362
I went to the gallery to check if we had changed our course. But no. The

44
00:03:54.363 --> 00:04:00.791
Nautilus was still cruising up the coast of Portugal. This day of waiting seemed to last forever. My

45
00:04:00.792 --> 00:04:05.045
supper was served in my room as usual, but I could eat little. At

46
00:04:05.046 --> 00:04:14.180
seven o'clock, there were still 120 minutes left. I couldn't sit quietly anymore so I went to see the gallery for the last time. I

47
00:04:14.181 --> 00:04:21.672
spent a long while admiring its treasures, and I wished the panels were open so I could gaze underwater one more time. A

48
00:04:21.673 --> 00:04:30.331
clock struck eight, startling me. I returned to my room and dressed warmly. I wasn't taking anything with me besides my notes. At

49
00:04:30.332 --> 00:04:37.489
a few minutes before nine, I pressed my ear to the door that linked my cabin and the captain's. Silence. I

50
00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:40.664
returned to the gallery and then went to the library. I

51
00:04:40.665 --> 00:04:45.243
stood at the door leading to the central staircase and awaited Ned's signal. Suddenly

52
00:04:45.244 --> 00:04:52.997
I heard the submarine's propeller slow down and stop. The only sound came from my heart beating loudly. I

53
00:04:52.998 --> 00:04:58.394
felt a slight shock and knew that the Nautilus had stopped on the sea bottom. Something

54
00:04:58.395 --> 00:05:02.156
was not right and there was no signal from Ned. "There

55
00:05:02.157 --> 00:05:06.616
you are!" Captain Nemo entered the library from the gallery. He

56
00:05:06.617 --> 00:05:13.465
was smiling broadly. "I've been looking all over for you, Professor. Do you know the history of Spain?" "What?"

57
00:05:13.466 --> 00:05:18.560
I was still startled by the captain's arrival in the midst of my escape. "I

58
00:05:18.561 --> 00:05:21.370
asked if you knew the history of Spain." Captain

59
00:05:21.371 --> 00:05:29.560
Nemo went on to describe a famous sea battle where Spanish ships, loaded with silver and gold, were sunk in 1702. "We're

60
00:05:29.561 --> 00:05:36.520
right at the shipwreck site. If you come with me, you'll likely be very interested in what I have to show you." By

61
00:05:36.521 --> 00:05:42.857
this point, I'd recovered my composure, so I followed him into the gallery, where the panels were now open. I

62
00:05:42.858 --> 00:05:45.703
looked out and saw a shipwreck. Members

63
00:05:45.704 --> 00:05:52.925
of the submarine's crew were carrying silver and gold bars as well as precious jewels back to the Nautilus .

64
00:05:52.926 --> 00:06:01.425
"This treasure, as well as other shipwrecks that I've marked on a map, are the source of my great wealth," Captain Nemo explained.

65
00:06:01.426 --> 00:06:05.012
"But doesn't this all belong to someone?" I asked.

66
00:06:05.013 --> 00:06:10.909
"The Spanish government? And even if it doesn't, aren't there poor people who could benefit from all these riches?"

67
00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.830
As soon as I spoke, I saw that I had wounded the captain.

68
00:06:14.831 --> 00:06:19.163
"Do you think I collect these treasures for myself?" asked Captain Nemo.

69
00:06:19.164 --> 00:06:27.909
"Do you think I'm ignorant of all the suffering people on this earth, miserable creatures who should be consoled, victims who should be avenged?"

70
00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:33.655
And then I understood for whom those gold bars in the Grecian islands were destined.

71
00:06:33.656 --> 00:06:42.794
My ruined escape plans seemed like such a small problem compared to the misery of those people who needed the captain's philanthropy.

